I am Nick Gillespie with Reason TV and  today we're talking to Rob Campea the  founder and head of Marijuana Policy  Project which is celebrating its 15th  anniversary Rob congratulations on that  what prompted you to start MPP 15 years  ago well 15 years ago there had really  been no one lobbying Congress in almost  two decades on the marijuana issue and  so since I was living in DC at the time  and I saw this gaping need  I started up MPP in January of 95 with  two friends of mine what's the absolute  mission of MPP the mission is to  regulate marijuana like alcohol you  recently in a in or in a recent press  release you talked about kind of 15  great things that happen have happened  in the past 15 years about Marijuana  Policy what's been good about the past  15 years well all the 13 state level  medical marijuana laws have been enacted  in the last 15 years so if you just try  to think back to 15 years ago medical  marijuana being illegal across the  entire country public support for making  marijuana legal for recreational use was  down in the high teens or low 20s so  things have changed not only with regard  to passing these state-level laws we  also passed a marijuana  decriminalization law in Massachusetts  with 65% of the vote and then the Obama  administration has been the the best  presidential administration on the  medical marijuana issue  well now that doesn't necessarily say  very much doesn't say very much now but  I think that was a big victory for our  cause because when you have the you know  US Attorney General saying that the DEA  and then and the Attorney General's  Office is not going to go after Medical  Marijuana providers in the states where  it's legal that then encourages more  activity on the medical marijuana issue  because they don't have that fear of the  federal thugs coming in and kicking in  their doors what what explains the shift  and certainly in medical marijuana I  mean kind of really starts in the in 96  right with California and Arizona  but what what explains the shift towards  a more progressive enlightened attitude  towards medical marijuana well I think  that the the public support for medical  marijuana has been in the high 70s for  many many years so I don't really see a  change in polling on that the reason  that we've had progress in the last 15  years is because we've actually had some  money to spend to take that public  support and to turn it into actual  policy change meaning we have money to  pay for lobbyists in Vermont or Rhode  Island or we have money to do a  signature drive in Montana or Michigan  is really just money that's converting  it into actual policy change what and  now the public opinion as its measured  in polls for legalization of marijuana  is also at an all-time high  what what do you think explains that  because it's not as if this is the late  70s and youth rates are climbing and you  know or where it's very kind of common  out in the public what explains a kind  of enthusiasm for legalizing or at least  decriminalizing marijuana  well the Gallup Organization which is  the only organizations that's done a  consistent Paul since 1969 has found  that making marijuana legal is how they  word it has hit the support level of 44  percent nationwide in 2009 which is a  far higher number than even in the 60s  of the 70s the reason that it's climbing  I think is a couple different things one  is the the proportion of the American  population that's having some personal  experience with marijuana either  personally or they've seen it is at a  higher level than ever before you know  in the 70s and you point out that you  know use was kind of rampant that's true  late 70s teenagers and college students  were smoking openly in public but the  proportion of the entire American  population that used at some point in  the 70s was lower than what we have  today so that demographic change each a  year with the population becoming more  comfortable with it I think translates  into support for making it legal and the  other thing I think that's happened is  that when you have credible institutions  and personalities talking about medical  marijuana and that being in the news a  lot that's changed the image of a  marijuana user from some you know  countercultural  unemployed hippie type  Aereo type to people who are sympathetic  you know grandma who has cancer or the  AIDS patient down the street or Montel  Williams with multiple sclerosis so I  think having a more accurate image of  marijuana users in pop culture is also  then I think turn the heat off a little  bit in terms of people's desire to  incarcerate who were the who are the  responsible drug users or marijuana  users that are not medical marijuana  users in in the public view that you  think help change the image of the pot  user from a kind of unemployed hippie  slacker to people who are you know  smoking pot the way that some people  drink martinis well you know it's it's  tough to really point to a few people I  think it's just the fact that there's so  many hundreds of opinion leaders or  celebrities that have actually come out  you know Carl Sagan was what was one I  think it was very important since he was  a well respected scientist and he was  using for recreational use I don't know  how much play that got I remember it  very clearly cuz I work on the issue but  and then you have high-level politicians  that I think is probably the most  important because it it it makes it  harder for them to be hypocritical at  least so you have Newt Gingrich who is  very open about his past use Clarence  Thomas and then you have the last three  presidents who have used marijuana with  regardless of how honest they want to be  about how they Ward their their answers  and you know and then of course almost  every Democratic presidential candidate  in the last couple of cycles has  admitted to past use so I think when you  have politicians who are the least  likely to admit admitting it makes it  easier for everyone else to admit and  you know what I think when they get  around to admitting future drug use then  it'll totally be time for legalization  Oh be a game-changer what is what is the  what are your hopes for the next 15  years of MPP what are what are the goals  that you want to see achieved well I  just actually finished writing our three  year strategic plan for 2010 to 2012 and  in that plan I sketch out a future where  in four years we could pass legislation  on the federal level that would get the  federal government out of medical  marijuana entirely so basically codify  the Obama administration's informal  policy and then making marijuana  completely legal in at least one state  in the neck  three years meaning California Colorado  or Nevada and that's for recreational or  medical use that's right that would be  complete treating it like alcohol in one  of those states so that's just the next  three years since you asked me about 15  years from now I fully intend on having  marijuana be completely legal in the  entire country 15 years from now what uh  what would be the benefits of legal  marijuana one is that instead of  spending an enormous amount of  government resources going after  marijuana users that it would stead we  would have legitimate businesses making  legitimate money and having employees  would be legal whether it's growing or  selling or packaging or whatever so  that's the money issue secondly with  eight hundred thousand plus marijuana  arrests a year almost all of which are  for simple possession  that's an enormous wear and tear on the  criminal justice system so by making  marijuana legal you're allowing the  police to focus on real crime you're  helping the the workload of the public  defender's courts being clogged judges  time and jail space so there's a the  whole criminal justice system would be  would be helped with it and with  marijuana in Massachusetts when we get  criminalized just for personal  possession we overnight reduce the  arrest rate for all offenses in  Massachusetts by six percent and so six  percent might not sound like a lot but  if you know if you're an overworked  public defender taking six percent of  your of your docket away would be a  really good thing  so there's all kinds of benefits not to  mention of course that if marijuana were  completely legal and regulated like  alcohol than people who need it for  medical purposes would just be able to  go down down to the store and get it  well I want to thank you and  congratulations on the first 15 years I  would hold a blunt up if I had one to  wish you well for the next 15 thank you  very much for reason TV I'm Nick  Gillespie and I was talking with Rob  Campea a founder of the Marijuana Policy  pro  you
 

